export const lessons = {
 math: [
  chapter('Matrices, determinants and eigenvalues','A matrix represents a linear map. An inverse exists when that map sends different inputs to different outputs. For a square matrix, the determinant tells you whether dimensions collapse. Expand a 3 × 3 determinant along the row or column with the most zeros. For an eigenvector, the matrix changes the magnitude or sign but preserves the vector’s direction. Find the eigenvalues first, then solve the resulting homogeneous equations for a nonzero vector.',['det','eig'],'For a diagonal matrix with entries 3 and −2, the determinant is −6 and the eigenvalues are 3 and −2. Its inverse exists because neither diagonal entry is zero.','A zero determinant means singular. It does not say every eigenvalue is zero.'),
  chapter('Probability and random variables','A random variable maps outcomes to numbers. A distribution tells you how likely the values are. For a count with fixed trials, test whether they are independent and whether the success probability stays the same before using a binomial distribution. Expectation is a weighted average. Variance measures spread around that average. Conditional probability updates the sample space when you know an event occurred.',['binom','bayes','stats'],'In three fair tosses, exactly two heads can occur in HHT, HTH or THH. Each sequence has probability 1/8, so the combined probability is 3/8.','Exactly two and at least two are different. The latter also includes three heads.'),
  chapter('Calculus and differential equations','A derivative gives the local slope and an integral accumulates a quantity over an interval. A Taylor expansion uses derivatives to estimate nearby values. A first-order differential equation describes how a quantity changes with time. For an equation with a forcing term, the total solution combines natural and forced responses. Use the initial condition to determine the constant in the natural response.',['taylor','ode'],'For dy/dt=−2y and y(0)=3, separate the variables to get ln(y)=−2t+C. The initial condition yields y=3e⁻²ᵗ.','A Taylor approximation is local. Check the expansion point and whether the change in input is small.')
 ],
 networks: [
  chapter('Circuit laws and equivalent sources','Start a DC or AC network problem by choosing a reference node. Use KCL at nodes or KVL around loops, then check signs based on your chosen current directions. To obtain a Thévenin equivalent, find the open-circuit voltage and the resistance or impedance seen at the terminals. Zero independent voltage sources by shorting them and independent current sources by opening them. Keep dependent sources active and use a test source if needed.',['kcl','kvl','conj'],'A 12 V ideal source in series with 3 Ω is already a Thévenin equivalent. Across a 3 Ω load, the current is 2 A and the load voltage is 6 V.','With a fixed real load in AC, do not assume full complex-conjugate matching is possible.'),
  chapter('Time response in RC and RL circuits','Capacitors store electric energy and inductors store magnetic energy. Without an impulse, capacitor voltage and inductor current are continuous through switching. Solve the circuit before the switch to get the initial value, after the switch for the final value, and find resistance seen by the storage element to get the time constant.',['time','trans'],'For a 10 V step into a series RC whose initial capacitor voltage is zero, vC starts at zero and reaches 6.32 V after one time constant.','Calculate the time constant from the post-switch circuit seen by the capacitor or inductor.'),
  chapter('Phasors, AC power and two-ports','Represent steady-state sinusoidal voltage and current by complex phasors. Resistance contributes a real term and inductance or capacitance a reactive term. The impedance angle gives the voltage phase relative to the current. For a cascade of two-ports, multiply the ABCD matrices in the order the signal passes through the sections.',['imp','rlphase','power','abcd'],'With R=10 Ω and XL=10 Ω, the impedance is 10+j10 Ω. Its angle is +45°, so current lags voltage by 45°.','A phasor magnitude may be peak or RMS depending on convention. Use RMS in the average-power equation.')
 ],
 signals: [
  chapter('Signals, impulse response and convolution','Signals are functions of continuous time or sample index. A linear time-invariant system obeys superposition and responds to a delayed input with an equally delayed output. Its impulse response captures the behavior for any input. Convolution weights shifted copies of that response and adds them to get the output.',['conv','lti'],'If h(t)=3δ(t−2), convolution selects x at t−2 and multiplies it by 3, giving y(t)=3x(t−2).','A delay changes t to t−T inside the signal. The later event appears at a larger value of t.'),
  chapter('Laplace, Fourier and Z transforms','Laplace transforms help solve continuous-time differential equations and circuits. The pole locations and region of convergence help identify causality and stability. Fourier transforms show the frequency content of a signal. The Z-transform plays the corresponding role for discrete-time sequences, with the unit circle corresponding to frequency response when it lies in the region of convergence.',['laplace','fourier','zshift'],'u(t−2) is a delayed step. Its transform has a factor e^(−2s). Multiplying it by the transform of t u(t) gives e^(−2s)/s³.','For a bilateral Laplace or Z-transform, the algebraic expression alone may not specify the signal without an ROC.'),
  chapter('Sampling and aliasing','Sampling takes evenly spaced measurements of a continuous-time signal. In frequency, sampling repeats the spectrum at multiples of the sampling rate. If the repeated spectra overlap, the samples cannot distinguish the original components. Use an anti-alias filter before the ADC and a sampling rate with room for the filter transition.',['sample'],'For cos(100πt), angular frequency is 100π rad/s, so f=50 Hz. If a second term is at 100 Hz, the highest frequency is 100 Hz and the ideal Nyquist rate is 200 samples/s.','The Nyquist rate is twice the signal bandwidth for low-pass bandlimited signals. The Nyquist frequency of a given sampler is half its sample rate.')
 ],
 devices: [
  chapter('Carriers, junctions and breakdown','Electrons and holes carry current. An electric field drives drift, while a concentration gradient drives diffusion. When p-type and n-type material touch, diffusion leaves behind charged dopants and creates the depletion region. Forward bias reduces the barrier and strongly increases current. A reverse-biased, heavily doped junction can undergo Zener tunneling through its narrow depletion region. Avalanche breakdown comes from impact ionization.',['thermal','shockley','einstein'],'An ideal diode at 300 K with ID/IS=10,000 needs VD≈25.85 mV × ln(10,001)≈0.238 V.','Check the region before using Shockley’s equation. Reverse breakdown requires another model.'),
  chapter('BJT and MOSFET operating regions','A BJT has cutoff, forward-active and saturation regions. In forward active, the base-emitter junction is forward biased and the base-collector junction is reverse biased. In saturation both are forward biased. A long-channel NMOS is off in the basic model below threshold. Above threshold, a small VDS gives triode operation, while VDS≥VGS−VTH gives saturation. MOS saturation generally models an amplifier current source.',['bjt','mos'],'An NMOS with VGS=2.0 V and VTH=0.8 V has 1.2 V overdrive. At VDS=0.5 V it is in triode. At VDS=1.5 V it is in saturation.','BJT saturation and MOSFET saturation describe different device behavior. State the device and check its terminal voltages.'),
  chapter('Light emission and detection','In a direct-bandgap semiconductor, electron–hole recombination can emit light. The emitted photon energy is near the transition energy. A photodiode can generate carriers when absorbed photons have sufficient energy. For an estimate, convert between photon energy and wavelength using the vacuum wavelength.',['photon'],'At 1240 nm the photon energy is about 1 eV. An ideal absorption threshold of 1.4 eV corresponds to a shorter wavelength near 886 nm.','Energy and wavelength are inversely related. A higher energy threshold corresponds to a shorter limiting wavelength.')
 ],
 analog: [
  chapter('Op-amp circuits and negative feedback','An ideal op-amp draws no input current. Negative feedback can hold its input voltage difference near zero while the output remains in its linear range. For an inverting stage, use KCL at the summing node. For a non-inverting stage, the feedback divider sets the gain. A real op-amp has output swing and bandwidth limits.',['inv','noninv','gbw'],'In an inverting stage with Rin=2 kΩ and Rf=10 kΩ, an input of +0.2 V ideally produces −1 V. Check that −1 V lies within the output swing.','Virtual ground is a voltage relation. The op-amp input still draws no current in the ideal model.'),
  chapter('Bias point and small-signal amplifiers','Find the transistor’s DC bias before replacing it with an incremental model. Transconductance measures how much output current changes for a small change in input voltage. In a common-source stage, extra drain current increases the resistor drop and lowers the drain voltage. In a source follower, the source moves in the same direction as the gate. A common-gate stage takes its input at the source, so its input resistance is finite.',['gm','cs','follower'],'If gm=2 mS and RD∥RL=5 kΩ, the common-source gain is about −10. A small 10 mV input variation produces a −100 mV output variation.','Use the effective AC load, which may include an external resistor in parallel with the stage load.'),
  chapter('PWM and the linear modulation region','Sinusoidal PWM compares a sinusoidal reference with a higher-frequency triangle. Adjusting the reference amplitude changes the fundamental output voltage. As the modulation index exceeds one, some switching pulses disappear near the waveform peaks and the fundamental no longer scales linearly. It eventually reaches a square-wave limit.',['pwm'],'A 0.8 V reference and 1 V triangle give ma=0.8. A 1.2 V reference gives ma=1.2 and enters overmodulation.','Crossing ma=1 marks the end of the linear formula. The fundamental can still increase beyond that point.')
 ],
 digital: [
  chapter('Number representation and Boolean logic','A fixed-width word has a fixed number of digit positions, including leading zeros. Radix complements support subtraction, and the base and word width must be specified. For combinational logic, start with a truth table, write the required minterms, then simplify algebraically or group adjacent cells in a Karnaugh map.',['complement','boolean'],'The two-digit complement of 47 is 53. For three digits, represent it as 047 and take 1000−47=953.','For a K-map, adjacency follows Gray code and wraps at opposite edges. Group sizes are powers of two.'),
  chapter('Flip-flops, state counts and timing','A flip-flop stores one bit. A combinational circuit depends on current inputs. A sequential circuit depends on current inputs and stored state. Draw the state sequence before choosing the number of flip-flops and reset logic. In a ripple counter, changes propagate between stages. In a synchronous counter, all stages share a clock.',['states','toggle','setup'],'A counter that visits 0 through 64 needs 65 states. Six bits give 64 states, so the counter needs seven bits.','Setup and hold test different timing paths. Setup uses a maximum-delay path and hold uses a minimum-delay path.'),
  chapter('Sampling, quantization and data rate','An ADC measures a sample and maps it to one of finitely many codes. Bit depth controls quantization step size, while sample rate controls how often the input is measured. Ideal full-scale sine SQNR improves about 6 dB per added bit. To compute raw output data rate, multiply bits per sample by samples per second.',['adc','sqnr'],'At 1 Msamples/s, a 10-bit ADC outputs 10 Mbit/s and its ideal full-scale sine SQNR is about 61.96 dB.','Sampling frequency and analog input frequency are different quantities. Use sampling frequency for the ADC output data rate.')
 ],
 control: [
  chapter('Feedback and the characteristic equation','A negative-feedback loop subtracts a measured output from the reference. The forward system acts on the resulting error. Write the closed-loop transfer function before finding poles. For a continuous-time rational system, poles strictly in the left half of the complex plane give decaying modes. Right-half-plane poles give growing modes. The Routh test can count right-half-plane roots without solving for them.',['loop','pid'],'For unity feedback and G=4/(s+2), the closed-loop pole is −6. Feedback moves the pole and changes the response speed.','Positive coefficients alone cannot prove stability for a polynomial of order three or higher.'),
  chapter('Transient response and steady-state tracking','A standard second-order system is characterized by natural frequency and damping ratio. Underdamped systems can overshoot and oscillate while the envelope decays. To estimate settling time, use the prescribed tolerance band. For steady-state error, identify the input type and apply the final-value theorem only when its stability conditions are met.',['second','settle','overshoot','error'],'For denominator s²+4s+25, ωn=5 rad/s and ζ=0.4. The 2% settling-time estimate is 4/(0.4×5)=2 s.','The coefficient of s is 2ζωn. Omitting the factor of two changes both damping and settling-time calculations.'),
  chapter('Frequency response and controller choice','A Bode plot shows how the open-loop gain and phase vary with frequency. Phase margin measures the extra lag at unity loop gain before reaching −180°. Controller terms reshape the loop. Integral action adds low-frequency gain, and derivative action can contribute damping. Check closed-loop stability for the resulting controller and plant.',['margin','pid'],'If the loop phase is −135° at the frequency where gain is 0 dB, the phase margin is 45°.','Check the loop transfer L(s) for margins. A positive margin at one crossover alone may be inadequate when there are multiple crossovers.')
 ],
 comm: [
  chapter('Analog modulation, symbols and pulse shaping','Modulation places information on a signal suitable for transmission. In conventional AM the envelope varies, while FM varies instantaneous frequency. Digital modulation maps bits into symbols. The number of symbols sent per second controls the pulse bandwidth, while the number of bits per symbol relates symbol rate to bit rate. Raised-cosine shaping trades excess bandwidth for practical pulse behavior.',['amfm','symbol','nyquist'],'16-QAM carries four bits per symbol. At 8 Mbit/s, the symbol rate is 2 Mbaud. Ideal zero-roll-off one-sided baseband bandwidth is 1 MHz.','Always identify whether the problem asks for one-sided baseband bandwidth or occupied passband bandwidth.'),
  chapter('Noise, SNR and detection','Noise density is specified per hertz. Integrate it over the receiver bandwidth to find the total noise power. Subtract noise power in dBm from signal power in dBm to obtain their ratio in dB. A receiver uses the noisy observation to decide which symbol was sent. Bit error probability depends on the modulation, receiver, channel and energy per bit.',['noise','db','ber'],'A density of −174 dBm/Hz over 1 MHz gives −114 dBm. A −80 dBm signal therefore gives 34 dB SNR.','dBm describes power relative to 1 mW. dB describes a ratio. Keep the units distinct until conversion.'),
  chapter('Information, channel capacity and efficiency','Entropy measures the average information of a discrete source. A channel capacity is the maximal reliable information rate under the model assumptions. Shannon’s formula for AWGN uses bandwidth and linear SNR. Spectral efficiency is rate divided by bandwidth. A practical system can fall below capacity because of modulation, coding, overhead and implementation losses.',['entropy','shannon'],'At 1 MHz and a linear SNR of 3, the capacity is 1 MHz × log₂(4)=2 Mbit/s, with 2 bit/s/Hz spectral efficiency.','The SNR in the logarithm must be linear. Convert 10 dB to 10 before applying Shannon’s formula.')
 ],
 em: [
  chapter('Wave quantities and antenna links','Electromagnetic waves carry electric and magnetic fields. Wavelength is propagation speed divided by frequency. In far-field free-space antenna links, spreading causes received power to decrease with the square of separation. Antenna gain accounts for direction and efficiency. For decibel link budgets, convert gains and losses to compatible power units first.',['wavelength','friis'],'At 300 MHz, the free-space wavelength is about 1 m. Doubling distance under Friis conditions reduces received power by roughly 6 dB.','Friis assumes far-field free-space conditions. Nearby objects, multipath and polarization mismatch change the received power.'),
  chapter('Transmission lines, mismatch and delivered power','A line has a characteristic impedance determined by its distributed electrical properties. A different load impedance launches a reflected wave. The reflected voltage ratio is Γ and the reflected power fraction is |Γ|² for a lossless line with real characteristic impedance. Electrical length changes the impedance seen at the input even though the load stays the same.',['gamma','delivered','vswr','zin'],'For a 50 Ω line and a 100 Ω load, Γ=1/3. About 11.1% of incident power reflects and about 88.9% is delivered.','Use the squared magnitude for power. The magnitude alone represents a voltage-wave ratio.'),
  chapter('Rectangular waveguides and TE modes','A guide supports discrete field patterns called modes. A TE mode has zero electric field along the propagation direction. Each mode has a cutoff frequency, below which it does not propagate. The dominant TE₁₀ mode of a rectangular guide has the lowest cutoff when a>b. At cutoff the TE wave impedance tends toward infinity from above.',['cutoff','te'],'If the broad wall is 0.28 inches, convert to 0.007112 m. TE₁₀ cutoff is 3×10⁸/(2×0.007112)≈21.09 GHz.','Use the broad internal dimension a, not the narrow dimension or the external metal size.')
 ],
 apt: [
  chapter('Quantitative aptitude','Translate each sentence into a quantity and label the units. A percentage needs a reference value. For averages, reconstruct the totals before merging groups. For work problems, express each rate as a fraction of a job per unit time. Check whether a result falls within sensible bounds.',['pct','mean','speed','work'],'If 60 km takes one hour and the next 60 km takes two hours, the average speed is 120/3=40 km/h.','An average of speeds works directly only for equal travel times. Use total distance divided by total time in general.'),
  chapter('Counting, reasoning and verbal questions','For counting, ask whether order and repetition matter. Check small cases before generalizing. For reasoning, separate the given conditions from conclusions and try a counterexample. For vocabulary, use prefixes, roots and context to narrow the options, then test the exact meaning.',['combo'],'Nocturnal means active at night. Diurnal means active during the day. The pair shares the idea of time of activity and differs in which part of the day.','For an antonym, seek an opposite on the same dimension. A loosely related word is insufficient.')
 ]
};

const before = (title, idea, refs, approach, trap) => ({title, idea, refs, approach, trap});
export const questionPrep = {
 'g26-1':before('Antonyms and meaning','An antonym expresses the opposite meaning on the same scale. Here the scale is time of activity. You need the meaning of the word, without a calculation.',[],'Identify whether the word refers to day or night, then match the opposite time.','Normal and abnormal describe typicality, which is a different idea.'),
 'g26-26':before('Determinants and singular matrices','The determinant is a scalar computed from a square matrix. A nonzero determinant makes the matrix invertible. Cofactor expansion expresses a 3 × 3 determinant using 2 × 2 determinants with alternating signs.',['det'],'Expand once to obtain an expression in a and b, then test each pair.','A zero in an individual matrix entry does not make the determinant zero.'),
 'g26-51':before('ABCD parameters and cascades','A two-port relates voltages and currents at its input and output. A series impedance contributes a voltage drop, while a shunt admittance adds branch current. ABCD matrices combine those effects in cascade order.',['abcd'],'Identify each series impedance and convert the middle resistor to admittance. Then check the units of B and C.','B is an impedance in ohms and C an admittance in siemens.'),
 'g25-30':before('Common-source and common-drain MOS stages','An ideal MOS gate draws zero DC current, giving effectively infinite low-frequency input resistance when the signal goes into the gate. The common-source stage inverts, while the common-drain stage is a positive-gain source follower.',['cs','follower'],'Identify which terminal receives the input and which terminal gives the output for each statement.','A common-gate input goes into the source terminal, where the incremental resistance is finite.'),
 'g25-36':before('Controller choice and closed-loop roots','The controller multiplies the plant in the open-loop path. Stability depends on the roots of the closed-loop characteristic equation. Integral control adds a pole at the origin, while positive derivative and proportional terms can give positive damping and stiffness in this example.',['loop','pid','second'],'Write one characteristic polynomial for each controller and check where its roots lie.','Check the roots of the full closed-loop polynomial, not only those of the plant.'),
 'i25-26':before('ADC resolution and quantization noise','Adding a bit roughly halves the quantization step. The noise power scales as the square of that step, so halving the step reduces noise power by a factor of four, or about 6 dB.',['adc','sqnr'],'Multiply the number of extra bits by about 6 dB, then add that change to the original SQNR.','Two extra bits improve SQNR by about 12 dB, not by a factor of two in dB.'),
 'i25-25':before('TE impedance above cutoff','A rectangular guide’s cutoff changes the longitudinal phase constant. For a TE mode, wave impedance grows as the operating frequency approaches cutoff from above, and approaches the medium impedance far above cutoff.',['cutoff','te'],'Use the TE wave impedance, which divides by the square-root frequency factor.','TM wave impedance has a different dependence on that factor.'),
 'g26-13':before('Laplace transforms of a delayed step and a ramp','A unit step is zero before it turns on. A causal ramp is zero before time zero and increases linearly after. A delay multiplies the Laplace transform by an exponential, and convolution in time corresponds to multiplication in the transform domain.',['laplace','conv'],'Transform each input separately and multiply the results.','The delay adds e^(−2s) to the numerator. It does not shift the pole from zero to s=2.'),
 'g26-14':before('Zener versus avalanche breakdown','A reverse-biased heavily doped PN junction has a narrow depletion layer and a strong electric field. That combination allows quantum tunneling. In avalanche breakdown, carriers gain kinetic energy and create more carriers by impact ionization.',[],'Identify the mechanism named by the junction operating region. This is a conceptual question.','Diffusion is concentration-driven transport. It does not explain the defining Zener breakdown mechanism.'),
 'g26-22':before('Nyquist bandwidth and symbol rate','A symbol is one waveform choice. In a four-level alphabet each symbol can represent two bits, but ideal pulse bandwidth depends on how quickly symbols are transmitted. Nyquist pulse shaping determines the minimum one-sided baseband bandwidth.',['symbol','nyquist'],'Use the symbol rate already given and set roll-off α to zero for the ideal minimum.','Do not multiply the symbol rate by two before applying the bandwidth equation.'),
 'g26-27':before('Counter states and binary storage','Each flip-flop stores one bit, so n flip-flops represent 2ⁿ distinct bit patterns. Count every legal state, then round the required number of bits upward.',['states'],'Convert the inclusive range to a number of states, then compare it with successive powers of two.','Counting from 0 to 64 includes both endpoints.'),
 'g26-33':before('Cutoff of the dominant rectangular-guide mode','In an air-filled rectangular guide, the broad-wall dimension fixes the dominant TE₁₀ cutoff. The other dimension does not appear for this mode because its mode index is zero.',['cutoff'],'Convert the broad wall to metres, use c/(2a), then convert hertz to gigahertz.','Use 1 inch = 0.0254 m. Keep both the factor of two and the frequency unit conversion.'),
 'g26-45':before('Decimal complements and word width','The radix complement of a number is the value that adds to it to reach the next power of the radix in a fixed-width word. For two decimal digits, that power is 100.',['complement'],'Use 10² and subtract the given number.','Subtracting from 99 would give the 9’s complement instead.'),
 'g26-55':before('From noise density to Shannon capacity','A per-hertz noise density is not the total noise in the channel. First use the bandwidth to get noise power, then calculate SNR in dB and convert it to linear. Shannon’s logarithm gives the spectral efficiency before multiplying by bandwidth.',['noise','db','shannon'],'Work in dBm for signal and noise, then use a linear ratio in the capacity equation.','Do not insert 34 straight into the Shannon logarithm. 34 dB is about 2512 as a linear ratio.'),
 'g25-44':before('ADC signal quality and output rate','For an ideal uniform ADC, finite bit depth adds quantization noise. The standard full-scale sine approximation gives SQNR from bit depth. The raw output rate is determined by the sampling clock and the bits in every sample.',['adc','sqnr'],'Compute SQNR and bit rate separately.','The 500 kHz input tone does not replace the 1 MHz sampling clock in the rate equation.'),
 'g25-63':before('The forward-biased diode equation','Forward current increases exponentially with the voltage across the junction. In the ideal model, the slope is controlled by the thermal voltage. A large forward-to-saturation current ratio means the exponential term is dominant.',['thermal','shockley'],'Isolate the exponential, take the natural logarithm and multiply by the thermal voltage.','Use a natural logarithm and convert both currents to the same units before forming the ratio.'),
 'g25-64':before('Line mismatch and absorbed power','A mismatch creates a reflected voltage wave. On a lossless line, incident power is split into reflected and delivered power at the load. Compute the complex voltage reflection coefficient first, then square its magnitude.',['gamma','delivered'],'Use the complex load in Γ, calculate |Γ|², and multiply the incident power by the fraction that remains.','A complex impedance still delivers average power when it has a positive real part.'),
 'i25-60':before('Angular frequency and Nyquist rate','The argument of a sinusoid can be written as ωt or 2πft. Convert each sinusoid to hertz, find the highest frequency, and double it for the ideal Nyquist rate.',['sample'],'Read the coefficients of t as angular frequencies, not frequencies in hertz.','Amplitudes do not set the Nyquist rate for these two sinusoids.'),
 'i25-39':before('Sinusoidal PWM and overmodulation','The modulation index is reference peak divided by carrier peak. Below one, the fundamental follows that ratio linearly. Above one, the comparison clips during parts of the cycle, so the output fundamental grows nonlinearly toward a limit.',['pwm'],'Compare both modulation-index values with one and identify which region each belongs to.','The end of linear modulation is not the point where the fundamental instantly stops increasing.'),
 'd08-4':before('Voltage-current phase in a series RL circuit','An ideal inductor has positive imaginary impedance. The resistor contributes a real term. The angle of the sum is the phase added when converting current to voltage, so a positive angle means current lags voltage.',['imp','rlphase'],'Compute inductive reactance in ohms and take the angle of R+jXL.','The question gives angular frequency already. Do not multiply by 2π again.'),
 'd08-6':before('Complex conjugate matching','A source with complex Thévenin impedance loses some voltage across its own impedance. Cancelling the reactive parts and matching the resistive parts maximizes the load’s average power when you can choose both parts of the load.',['conj'],'Keep the source resistance and reverse the sign of its reactance.','Matching the entire complex impedance leaves the two reactances with the same sign.')
};

